  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and the rule reads back as enabled.\n\n1. **Data view 'Kibana Sample Data Logs'** (`kibana_sample_data_logs`): the time field is `timestamp`. Key fields for this work are `timestamp` (date), `response` (string, with `response.keyword` for exact matching), and `clientip`, `url`, `bytes`, and `agent`.\n\n2. **Dashboard 'S4-5c1d45fd Web logs'** (ID `f13a1a9b-288b-4d4c-ba38-daeaa823123d`) has exactly two panels, both line/bar charts backed by a data-view reference to the sample data view:\n   - \"Requests over time\": a line chart of count by date histogram on `timestamp`.\n   - \"Requests by response code\": a bar chart of count by terms on `response.keyword`.\n\n3. **Alert rule 'S4-5c1d45fd 5xx errors'** (ID `b869be97-648c-41ae-b0ee-8eaa7eeccaa5`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on the data view. It uses the KQL filter `response.keyword >= \"500\" and response.keyword <= \"599\"` and fires when the count is greater than 10 over a 5-minute window. It runs every 1 minute. It was created enabled, so no enable call was needed.\n\n4. **Panel rename**: the response-code panel is now titled 'Responses by code'. The other panel is unchanged. The rule read back with `enabled: true`, and its execution status is `ok`.\n\nChoices and caveats:\n- The data view has no numeric response field, so the 5xx range is a lexical comparison on the keyword string. The index only has 200, 404, and 503 values, so the rule's matches today are all 503s. That is correct for the range, but I did not run the rule's evaluation to see whether it fires.\n- The ES|QL endpoints are not exposed on this Kibana, so I could not run the ES|QL queries directly. I used data-view layers for both panels, which the API validated.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
